WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.404
A routine check of a real business domain found one misspelled word in its DMARC record.

2
00:00:06.754 --> 00:00:11.619
The business is not named, and every value shown is a documentation example.

3
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.938
This is the record, read from public DNS.

4
00:00:17.288 --> 00:00:20.750
One tag stands out: s p equals quarentine.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.595
The other tags are valid.

6
00:00:22.945 --> 00:00:31.295
But s p accepts only three values: none, quarantine, or reject. Quarentine, with an e, is not one of them.

7
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.144
What does the standard say?

8
00:00:35.494 --> 00:00:46.054
RFC 7489 says that when the s p tag is not valid, and a report address is present, the receiver should act as if the policy were p equals none.

9
00:00:46.404 --> 00:00:51.919
So a policy written as quarantine can, under a strict reading, become monitor only.

10
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:55.592
Accuracy matters here.

11
00:00:55.942 --> 00:00:59.939
It does weaken protection against forged mail in the company's name.

12
00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:07.305
It does make the domain look less carefully managed, at a time when mailbox providers require valid DMARC.

13
00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:15.343
But it does not, on its own, explain why genuine mail lands in spam. Reputation and content decide most of that.

14
00:01:15.693 --> 00:01:21.431
Large receivers may ignore only the bad tag. The typo is one factor, not the whole story.

15
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:25.184
Why should the business care?

16
00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:28.254
Receipts and newsletters in spam are not read.

17
00:01:28.604 --> 00:01:35.898
Gmail and Yahoo have enforced sender rules since February twenty twenty four, and Microsoft since May twenty twenty five.

18
00:01:36.248 --> 00:01:40.605
A weak policy makes forged invoices in the company's name easier to deliver.

19
00:01:40.955 --> 00:01:44.510
Bulk complaints lower delivery for all of the domain's mail.

20
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.066
And the fix takes minutes, costs nothing, and can be undone.

21
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.386
The corrections.

22
00:01:52.736 --> 00:01:54.719
First, fix the spelling.

23
00:01:55.069 --> 00:02:02.406
Then move S P F to a hard fail once the reports are clean, and confirm DKIM signing in every sending service.

24
00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:07.682
Register with Google Postmaster Tools, and move bulk mail to its own subdomain.

25
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.676
Spell every tag exactly, check from the outside, and read the reports.
